<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<subsection xml:id="subsection-divergence-to-infinity" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
  <title>
    Divergence to Infinity
  </title>

  <introduction>
    <p>
      In some ways, the binary distinction between convergence and divergence may be viewed as more coarse than an analyst might like. On the real line <m> \R </m>, we can make more qualitative statements about the limiting behavior of certain divergent sequences and maps to alleviate this issue somewhat. We now consider the special case of divergence of a sequence or map to infinity. Some sequences increase or decrease without bound. Such sequences can be said to have infinite limits, even though they don't converge to any real number.
    </p>
  </introduction>
  
  <definition xml:id="definition-sequential-divergence-to-infinity">
    <title>
      Sequential divergence to infinity
    </title>
    
    <statement>
      <p>
        Let <m> \of{ x_{ n } }_{ n = 0 }^{ \infty } </m> be a sequence of real numbers <m> x_{ n } \in \R </m>.
        
        <ul>
          <li>
            <title>
              Divergence to <m> \infty </m>
            </title>
            
            <p>
              The sequence <m> \of{ x_{ n } }_{ n = 0 }^{ \infty } </m> is said to <term> diverge to <m> \infty </m> </term> if for each real number <m> B \in \R </m>, there is a natural number <m> n_{ B } \in \N </m> so that <m> x_{ n } \gt B </m> for all indices <m> n \geq n_{ B } </m>. In this case, we write
              
              <md>
                \lim_{ n \to \infty }{ x_{ n } } = \infty
              </md>.
            </p>
          </li>
          
          <li>
            <title>
              Divergence to <m> -\infty </m>
            </title>
            
            <p>
              The sequence <m> \of{ x_{ n } }_{ n = 0 }^{ \infty } </m> is said to <term> diverge to <m> -\infty </m> </term> if for each real number <m> B \in \R </m>, there is a natural number <m> n_{ B } \in \N </m> so that <m> x_{ n } \lt B </m> for all indices <m> n \geq n_{ B } </m>. In this case, we write
              
              <md>
                \lim_{ n \to \infty }{ x_{ n } } = -\infty
              </md>.
            </p>
          </li>
        </ul>
      </p>
    </statement>
  </definition>

  <example xml:id="example-sequential-divergence-to-infinity">
    <title>
      Sequential divergence to infinity
    </title>
    
    <statement>
      <p>
        The following are examples and non-examples of sequences of real numbers which diverge to infinity.
        
        <ul>
          <li>
            <p>
              The sequence <m> \of{ n }_{ n = 0 }^{ \infty } = \of{ 0 , 1 , 2 , 3 , \dotsc } </m> diverges to <m> \infty </m>; that is,
              
              <md>
                \lim_{ n \to \infty }{ n } = \infty
              </md>.
            </p>
          </li>
          
          <li>
            <p>
              The sequence <m> \of{ \of{ -1 }^{ n } n }_{ n = 0 }^{ \infty } = \of{ 0 , -1 , 2 , -3 , \dotsc } </m> diverges, but it does not diverge to <m> \infty </m> or <m> -\infty </m>.
            </p>
          </li>
        </ul>
      </p>
    </statement>
  </example>
  
  <p>
    There are also versions of divergence to infinity for real-valued functions on metric spaces.
  </p>
  
  <definition xml:id="definition-function-divergence-to-infinity">
    <title>
      Function divergence to infinity
    </title>
    
    <statement>
      <p>
        Let <m> f \colon X \to \R </m> be a real-valued function on a metric space <m> \of{ X , \rho } </m>.
        
        <ul>
          <li>
            <title>
              Divergence to <m> \infty </m>
            </title>
            
            <p>
              The function <m> f </m> is said to <term> diverge to <m> \infty </m> </term> at a point <m> c \in X </m> if for each real number <m> B \in \R </m>, there is a positive real number <m> \delta \gt 0 </m> so that <m> f \of{ x } \gt B </m> for all points <m> x \in X </m> so that <m> 0 \lt \rho \of{ x , c } \lt \delta </m>.
            </p>
            
            <p>
              In this case, if <m> c </m> is a limit point of <m> X </m>, then we write
              
              <md>
                \lim_{ x \to c }{ f \of{ x } } = \infty
              </md>.
            </p>
          </li>
          
          <li>
            <title>
              Divergence to <m> -\infty </m>
            </title>
            
            <p>
              The function <m> f </m> is said to <term> diverge to <m> -\infty </m> </term> at a point <m> c \in X </m> if for each real number <m> B \in \R </m>, there is a positive real number <m> \delta \gt 0 </m> so that <m> f \of{ x } \lt B </m> for all points <m> x \in X </m> so that <m> 0 \lt \rho \of{ x , c } \lt \delta </m>.
            </p>
            
            <p>
              In this case, if <m> c </m> is a limit point of <m> X </m>, then we write
              
              <md>
                \lim_{ x \to c }{ f \of{ x } } = -\infty
              </md>.
            </p>
          </li>
        </ul>
      </p>
    </statement>
  </definition>

  <example xml:id="example-function-divergence-to-infinity">
    <title>
      Function divergence to infinity
    </title>
    
    <statement>
      <p>
        The following are examples and non-examples of real-valued functions on metric spaces which diverge to infinity
        
        <ul>
          <li>
            <p>
              Consider the real-valued function <m> f \colon \R \setminus \set{ 1 } \to \R </m> defined by the formula
              
              <md>
                f \of{ x } = -\frac{ 1 }{ \of{ x - 1 }^{ 2 } }
              </md>.
              
              Then <m> f </m> diverges to <m> -\infty </m> at <m> 1 </m>; that is,
              
              <md>
                \lim_{ x \to 1 }{ -\frac{ 1 }{ x^{ 2 } } } = -\infty
              </md>.
            </p>
          </li>
          
          <li>
            <p>
              Consider the real-valued function <m> g \colon \R \setminus \set{ 0 } \to \R </m> defined by the formula
              
              <md>
                g \of{ y } = \frac{ 1 }{ y }
              </md>.
              
              Then <m> g </m> diverges at <m> 0 </m>, but it does not diverge to <m> \infty </m> or <m> -\infty </m> at <m> 0 </m>.
            </p>
          </li>
        </ul>
      </p>
    </statement>
  </example>
  
  <p>
    Divergence of a sequence of real numbers or a real-valued function to infinity is a strong statement about the limiting behavior of such a sequence or function. However, since there is no metric on the extended real number system which agrees with the Euclidean metric on the real numbers, we cannot view divergence to infinity as convergence in a metric space of which the real line is a metric subspace. We will see in <xref ref="chapter-topological-spaces"/> a way in which the situation of divergence to infinity can be said to be a form of convergence to an extended real number, namely the infinite quantities <m> \infty </m> and <m> -\infty </m>.
  </p>
  
  <p>
    In many ways, the symbols <m> \infty </m> and <m> -\infty </m> can be viewed as one-sided <q> reciprocals </q> of <m> 0 </m>. While division by zero is left undefined for very important arithmetical reasons, it turns out that divergence of a sequence of nonzero real numbers to infinity implies the Euclidean convergence of the reciprocals to zero.
  </p>
  
  <proposition xml:id="proposition-reciprocals-of-infinite-limits">
    <title>
      Reciprocals of infinite limits
    </title>
    
    <statement>
      <p>
        Let <m> \of{ x_{ n } }_{ n = 0 }^{ \infty } </m> be a sequence of positive real numbers <m> x_{ n } \gt 0 </m>. Then
        
        <md>
          \lim_{ n \to \infty }{ x_{ n } } = \infty
        </md>
        
        if and only if
        
        <md>
          \lim_{ n \to \infty }{ \frac{ 1 }{ x_{ n } } } = 0
        </md>.
      </p>
    </statement>
    
    <proof>
      <p>
        First suppose that <m> \of{ x_{ n } }_{ n = 0 }^{ \infty } </m> diverges to <m> \infty </m>, and let <m> \epsilon \gt 0 </m>. Then <m> \frac{ 1 }{ \epsilon } \in \R </m> is a real number, and so there is a natural number <m> n_{ \epsilon } \in \N </m> so that
        
        <md>
          x_{ n } \gt \frac{ 1 }{ \epsilon }
        </md>
        
        for all indices <m> n \geq n_{ \epsilon } </m>. In particular,
        
        <md>
          d \of{ \frac{ 1 }{ x_{ n } } , 0 } = \abs{ \frac{ 1 }{ x_{ n } } - 0 } = \abs{ \frac{ 1 }{ x_{ n } } } = \frac{ 1 }{ x_{ n } } \lt \epsilon
        </md>
        
        for all indices <m> n \geq n_{ \epsilon } </m>, and so <m> \of{ \frac{ 1 }{ x_{ n } } }_{ n = 0 }^{ \infty } </m> converges to <m> 0 </m>.
      </p>
      
      <p>
        Conversely, now suppose that <m> \of{ \frac{ 1 }{ x_{ n } } }_{ n = 0 }^{ \infty } </m> converges to <m> 0 </m>, and let <m> B \in \R </m> be a real number. If <m> B \leq 0 </m> is non-positive, then
        
        <md>
          x_{ n } \gt 0 \geq B
        </md>
        
        for all indices <m> n \in \N </m>. On the other hand, if <m> B \gt 0 </m> is positive, then <m> \frac{ 1 }{ B } \gt 0 </m> is a positive real number, and so there is a natural number <m> n_{ B } </m> so that
        
        <md>
          d \of{ \frac{ 1 }{ x_{ n } } , 0 } \lt \frac{ 1 }{ B }
        </md>
        
        for all indices <m> n \geq n_{ B } </m>. In particular,
        
        <md>
          x_{ n } = \abs{ x_{ n } } = \frac{ 1 }{ \abs{ \frac{ 1 }{ x_{ n } } - 0 } } = \frac{ 1 }{ d \of{ \frac{ 1 }{ x_{ n } } , 0 } } \gt B
        </md>
        
        for all indices <m> n \geq n_{ B } </m>, and so <m> \of{ x_{ n } }_{ n = 0 }^{ \infty } </m> diverges to <m> \infty </m>.
      </p>
    </proof>
  </proposition>
  
  <p>
    So, as far as limiting behavior of sequences of real numbers goes, the infinite quantities <m> \infty </m> and <m> -\infty </m> behave like one-sided reciprocals of zero. It's incredibly important to avoid assuming anything stronger than what is explicitly stated in the proposition above. Specifically, the hypothesis that the sequence in question have positive real number values is crucial, and the statement is not necessarily true without this requirement.
  </p>
  
  <p>
    A sequence of real numbers diverges to <m> \infty </m> or <m> -\infty </m> if it increases or decreases, respectively, without bound. This notion of <term> boundedness </term> is directly related to the one introduced in <xref ref="section-introduction-to-order-theory"/> for subsets of partially ordered sets. Informally, a map with codomain a partially ordered set is considered to be bounded if its image is bounded.
  </p>
  
  <definition xml:id="definition-map-boundedness">
    <title>
      Map boundedness
    </title>
    
    <idx>
      boundedness
    </idx>
    
    <notation>
      <usage>
        <m>  </m>
      </usage>
      
      <description>
        <p>
          
        </p>
      </description>
    </notation>
    
    <statement>
      <p>
        Let <m> f \colon X \to Y </m> be a map from a set <m> X </m> to a partially ordered set <m> \of{ Y , \preceq } </m>.
        
        <ul>
          <li>
            <title>
              Upper bound
            </title>
            
            <p>
              An output <m> y \in Y </m> is an <term> upper bound </term> for the map <m> f </m> if it is an upper bound for the image <m> \im \of{ f } = f \of{ X } </m>; that is, <m> y </m> is an upper bound for <m> f </m> if <m> f \of{ x } \preceq y </m> for all inputs <m> x \in X </m>.
            </p>
            
            <p>
              The map <m> f </m> is called <term> bounded from above </term> if there is an upper bound for <m> f </m>, and <term> unbounded from above </term> if there is no such upper bound.
            </p>
          </li>
          
          <li>
            <title>
              Lower bound
            </title>
            
            <p>
              An output <m> y \in Y </m> is a <term> lower bound </term> for the map <m> f </m> if it is a lower bound for the image <m> \im \of{ f } = f \of{ X } </m>; that is, <m> y </m> is a lower bound for <m> f </m> if <m> y \preceq f \of{ x } </m> for all inputs <m> x \in X </m>.
            </p>
            
            <p>
              The map <m> f </m> is called <term> bounded from below </term> if there is a lower bound for <m> f </m>, and <term> unbounded from below </term> if there is no such lower bound.
            </p>
          </li>
          
          <li>
            <title>
              Boundedness
            </title>
            
            <p>
              The map <m> f </m> is called <term> bounded </term> if it is both bounded from above and bounded from below, and <m> f </m> is called <term> unbounded </term> otherwise if it is either unbounded from above or unbounded from below (or both).
            </p>
          </li>
        </ul>
      </p>
    </statement>
  </definition>
  
  <example xml:id="example-sequential-boundedness-in-the-real-line">
    <title>
      Sequential boundedness in the real line
    </title>
    
    <statement>
      <p>
        A sequence <m> \of{ x_{ n } }_{ n = 0 }^{ \infty } </m> of real numbers <m> x_{ n } \in \R </m> is bounded if and only if there are real numbers <m> a , b \in \R </m> such that <m> a \leq x_{ n } \leq b </m> for all indices <m> n \in \N </m>. For example, the sequence <m> \of{ \frac{ 1 }{ n } }_{ n = 1 }^{ \infty } </m> is bounded, because
        
        <md>
          0 \lt \frac{ 1 }{ n } \leq 1
        </md>
        
        for all indices <m> n \geq 1 </m>.
      </p>
    </statement>
  </example>
  
  <p>
    Clearly, any sequence of real numbers or any real-valued function which diverges to infinity cannot be bounded. However, the converse is not true; unbounded sequences need not diverge to infinity. We have already seen one such example of this in <xref ref="example-function-divergence-to-infinity"/>.
  </p>
  
  <p>
    Considered as a real-valued function on the set <m> \N </m> of natural numbers, a sequence of real numbers is a map whose codomain is a totally ordered set. However, the domain of such a sequence is also totally ordered. Because the metric structure on the real line <m> \R </m> can be described in terms of the standard ordering <m> \leq </m>, sequences which preserve these orders have special convergence properties. Such sequences are called <term> monotone </term>.
  </p>
  
  <definition xml:id="definition-monotonicity">
    <title>
      Monotonicity
    </title>
    
    <idx>
      non-decreasing
    </idx>
    
    <idx>
      order-preserving
    </idx>
    
    <idx>
      isotonicity
    </idx>
    
    <idx>
      increasing
    </idx>
    
    <idx>
      strictly order-preserving
    </idx>
    
    <idx>
      strict isotonicity
    </idx>
    
    <idx>
      non-increasing
    </idx>
    
    <idx>
      order-reversing
    </idx>
    
    <idx>
      antitonicity
    </idx>
    
    <idx>
      decreasing
    </idx>
    
    <idx>
      strictly order-reversing
    </idx>
    
    <idx>
      strict antitonicity
    </idx>
    
    <idx>
      monotonicity
    </idx>
    
    <idx>
      strict monotonicity
    </idx>
    
    <statement>
      <p>
        Let <m> f \colon X \to Y </m> be a map from a partially ordered set <m> \of{ X , \preceq_{ X } } </m> to a partially ordered set <m> \of{ Y , \preceq_{ Y } } </m>.
        
        <ul>
          <li>
            <title>
              Isotonicity
            </title>
            
            <p>
              The map <m> f </m> is called <term> non-decreasing </term>, <term> order-preserving </term>, or <term> isotone </term> if for all inputs <m> x_{ 1 } , x_{ 2 } \in X </m>, if <m> x_{ 1 } \preceq_{ X } x_{ 2 } </m>, then <m> f \of{ x_{ 1 } } \preceq_{ Y } f \of{ x_{ 2 } } </m>. The map <m> f </m> is called <term> increasing </term>, <term> strictly order-preserving </term>, or <term> strictly isotone </term> if it is non-decreasing and injective.
            </p>
          </li>
          
          <li>
            <title>
              Antitonicity
            </title>
            
            <p>
              The map <m> f </m> is called <term> non-increasing </term>, <term> order-reversing </term>, or <term> antitone </term> if for all inputs <m> x_{ 1 } , x_{ 2 } \in X </m>, if <m> x_{ 1 } \preceq_{ X } x_{ 2 } </m>, then <m> f \of{ x_{ 2 } } \preceq_{ Y } f \of{ x_{ 1 } } </m>. The map <m> f </m> is called <term> decreasing </term>, <term> strictly order-reversing </term>, or <term> strictly antitone </term> if it is non-increasing and injective.
            </p>
          </li>
          
          <li>
            <title>
              Monotonicity
            </title>
            
            <p>
              The map <m> f </m> is called <term> monotone </term> if it is either non-decreasing or non-increasing, and <m> f </m> is called <term> strictly monotone </term> if it is either increasing or decreasing.
            </p>
          </li>
        </ul>
      </p>
    </statement>
  </definition>
  
  <p>
    It turns out that all monotone sequences of real numbers have limits, in the sense that a monotone sequence of real numbers either converges to a real number or diverges to infinity. In fact, something slightly stronger is true; the limit of a non-decreasing sequence exists and is its supremum, and the limit of a non-increasing sequence exists and is its infimum. This useful fact is a property of the extended real number system <m> \R </m>. Other totally ordered number subsystems, such as the rational numbers <m> \Q </m>, do not enjoy this property, since suprema and infima of sets need not exist in these number systems, which are Dedekind incomplete in the sense introduced in <xref ref="section-the-complete-ordered-field"/>.
  </p>
  
  <theorem xml:id="theorem-monotone-convergence-theorem">
    <title>
      Monotone convergence theorem
    </title>
    
    <statement>
      <p>
        Let <m> \of{ x_{ n } }_{ n = 0 }^{ \infty } </m> be a sequence of real numbers <m> x_{ n } \in \R </m>.
        
        <ol>
          <li>
            <p>
              If <m> \of{ x_{ n } }_{ n = 0 }^{ \infty } </m> is non-decreasing, then
              
              <md>
                \lim_{ n \to \infty }{ x_{ n } } = \sup_{ n \in \N }{ x_{ n } }
              </md>.
            </p>
          </li>
          
          <li>
            <p>
              If <m> \of{ x_{ n } }_{ n = 0 }^{ \infty } </m> is non-increasing, then
              
              <md>
                \lim_{ n \to \infty }{ x_{ n } } = \inf_{ n \in \N }{ x_{ n } }
              </md>.
            </p>
          </li>
        </ol>
        
        In particular, if <m> \of{ x_{ n } }_{ n = 0 }^{ \infty } </m> is bounded and monotone, then it is convergent.
      </p>
    </statement>
    
    <proof>
      <ol>
        <li>
          <p>
            We observe that <m> \set{ x_{ n } }_{ n = 0 }^{ \infty } \neq \emptyset </m> is non-empty, and so <m> \sup_{ n \in \N }{ x_{ n } } = \sup \of{ \set{ x_{ n } }_{ n = 0 }^{ \infty } } \neq -\infty </m>. There are two cases; either <m> \sup_{ n \in \N }{ x_{ n } } = \infty </m> or <m> \sup_{ n \in \N }{ x_{ n } } \in \R </m> is a real number.
          </p>
          
          <p>
            First suppose that <m> \sup_{ n \in \N }{ x_{ n } } = \infty </m>, and let <m> B \in \R </m> be a real number. Since
            
            <md>
              B \lt \infty = \sup_{ n \in \N }{ x_{ n } }
            </md>,
            
            <m> B </m> is not an upper bound for <m> \set{ x_{ n } }_{ n = 0 }^{ \infty } </m>; that is, <m> x_{ n_{ B } } \gt B </m> for some natural number <m> n_{ B } \in \N </m>. We observe that since <m> \of{ x_{ n } }_{ n = 0 }^{ \infty } </m> is non-decreasing,
            
            <md>
              x_{ n } \geq x_{ n_{ B } } \gt B
            </md>
            
            for all indices <m> n \geq n_{ B } </m>, and so
            
            <md>
              \lim_{ n \to \infty }{ x_{ n } } = \infty = \sup_{ n \in \N }{ x_{ n } }
            </md>.
          </p>
          
          <p>
            On the other hand, now suppose that <m> S = \sup_{ n \in \N }{ x_{ n } } \in \R </m> is a real number, and let <m> \epsilon \gt 0 </m> be a positive real number. Since
            
            <md>
              S - \epsilon \lt S = \sup_{ n \in \N }{ x_{ n } }
            </md>,
            
            <m> S - \epsilon </m> is not an upper bound for <m> \set{ x_{ n } }_{ n = 0 }^{ \infty } </m>; that is, <m> x_{ n_{ \epsilon } } \gt S - \epsilon </m> for some natural number <m> n_{ \epsilon } \in \N </m>. Since <m> \of{ x_{ n } }_{ n = 0 }^{ \infty } </m> is non-decreasing,
            
            <md>
              S - \epsilon \lt x_{ n_{ \epsilon } } \leq x_{ n } \leq S
            </md>
            
            for all indices <m> n \geq n_{ \epsilon } </m>, and so
            
            <md>
              <mrow>
                d \of{ x_{ n } , S } \amp = \abs{ x_{ n } - S } = S - x_{ n }
              </mrow>
              
              <mrow>
                \amp \lt S - \of{ S - \epsilon } = \epsilon
              </mrow>
            </md>
            
            for all indices <m> n \geq n_{ \epsilon } </m>. We conclude that
            
            <md>
              \lim_{ n \to \infty }{ x_{ n } } = S = \sup_{ n \in \N }{ x_{ n } }
            </md>.
          </p>
        </li>
        
        <li>
          <p>
            We observe that <m> \set{ x_{ n } }_{ n = 0 }^{ \infty } \neq \emptyset </m> is non-empty, and so <m> \inf_{ n \in \N }{ x_{ n } } = \inf \of{ \set{ x_{ n } }_{ n = 0 }^{ \infty } } \neq \infty </m>. There are two cases; either <m> \inf_{ n \in \N }{ x_{ n } } = -\infty </m> or <m> \inf_{ n \in \N }{ x_{ n } } \in \R </m> is a real number.
          </p>
          
          <p>
            First suppose that <m> \inf_{ n \in \N }{ x_{ n } } = -\infty </m>, and let <m> B \in \R </m> be a real number. Since
            
            <md>
              B \gt -\infty = \inf_{ n \in \N }{ x_{ n } }
            </md>,
            
            <m> B </m> is not a lower bound for <m> \set{ x_{ n } }_{ n = 0 }^{ \infty } </m>; that is, <m> x_{ n_{ B } } \lt B </m> for some natural number <m> n_{ B } \in \N </m>. We observe that since <m> \of{ x_{ n } }_{ n = 0 }^{ \infty } </m> is non-increasing,
            
            <md>
              x_{ n } \leq x_{ n_{ B } } \lt B
            </md>
            
            for all indices <m> n \geq n_{ B } </m>, and so
            
            <md>
              \lim_{ n \to \infty }{ x_{ n } } = -\infty = \inf_{ n \in \N }{ x_{ n } }
            </md>.
          </p>
          
          <p>
            On the other hand, now suppose that <m> I = \inf_{ n \in \N }{ x_{ n } } \in \R </m> is a real number, and let <m> \epsilon \gt 0 </m> be a positive real number. Since
            
            <md>
              I + \epsilon \gt S = \inf_{ n \in \N }{ x_{ n } }
            </md>,
            
            <m> I + \epsilon </m> is not a lower bound for <m> \set{ x_{ n } }_{ n = 0 }^{ \infty } </m>; that is, <m> x_{ n_{ \epsilon } } \lt I + \epsilon </m> for some natural number <m> n_{ \epsilon } \in \N </m>. Since <m> \of{ x_{ n } }_{ n = 0 }^{ \infty } </m> is non-increasing,
            
            <md>
              I + \epsilon \gt x_{ n_{ \epsilon } } \geq x_{ n } \geq I
            </md>
            
            for all indices <m> n \geq n_{ \epsilon } </m>, and so
            
            <md>
              <mrow>
                d \of{ x_{ n } , I } \amp = \abs{ x_{ n } - I } = x_{ n } - I
              </mrow>
              
              <mrow>
                \amp \lt I + \epsilon - I = \epsilon
              </mrow>
            </md>
            
            for all indices <m> n \geq n_{ \epsilon } </m>. We conclude that
            
            <md>
              \lim_{ n \to \infty }{ x_{ n } } = I = \inf_{ n \in \N }{ x_{ n } }
            </md>.
          </p>
        </li>
      </ol>
    </proof>
  </theorem>

  <conclusion>
    <p>
      What we have just shown will prove incredibly useful; in particular, we will use the fact that all bounded, monotone sequences of real numbers are convergent several times throughout this chapter. The converse is not true; while convergent sequences of real numbers are bounded, not all such sequences need be monotone.
    </p>
  </conclusion>
</subsection>
